   ["AWS", "What is an EC2 placement group?", "placement-group", "Explain clustering.", "**Direct answer :** An EC2 placement group controls how instances are physically positioned relative to one another. AWS supports Cluster for low-latency/high-throughput communication within an Availability Zone, Spread for placing a small number of critical instances on distinct hardware, Partition for isolating groups across separate racks, and Precision time for supported time-sensitive workloads.\n\n**Why this is asked :** This tests whether you understand the AWS concept itself, not just its definition. The interviewer wants to see whether you can explain explain clustering. in practical terms and connect it to a real cloud architecture.\n\n**Breaking it down :** Think of placement group as an AWS building block rather than an isolated feature. An EC2 placement group controls how instances are physically positioned relative to one another. AWS supports Cluster for low-latency/high-throughput communication within an Availability Zone, Spread for placing a small number of critical instances on distinct hardware, Partition for isolating groups across separate racks, and Precision time for supported time-sensitive workloads. The important point is to explain what problem it solves, how it fits into an AWS architecture, and what limitations or trade-offs should influence the design.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :** A strong spoken answer should be: give the definition first, explain how it works, give one practical example, and finish with the main limitation or trade-off. This keeps the response concise while showing real implementation knowledge.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :** Be ready for a follow-up about the security, reliability, scaling, monitoring, and cost implications of the choice. A senior-level answer should also explain what happens during failure or traffic growth and how you would validate the design with AWS monitoring and operational data."]